# Native routed execution (Claude Code)
%% codex
# Native routed execution (Codex)
%% end

**Task:** $ARGUMENTS

The task can be a description, a plan just agreed in conversation, a file path, or a ticket. Reply in the user's language.

## Roles

| Role | Does | Never does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| This session (orchestrator) | Designs the plan: goal, boundaries, architecture, trade-offs, acceptance criteria. Splits work, writes briefs, dispatches, checks results, wraps up. | Explore, research or implement the task itself, or hand design to another agent. |
| `worker` | Read-only exploration, web research, implementation, merging, review fixes. | Review its own work. |
| `reviewer` | Read-only review and hard diagnosis; its model usually also sits on the committee. | Write project files. |

%% cc
Every role is a subagent started with the Agent tool. Which model plays each role is resolved in [models](references/models.md). Read a reference when the step that names it comes up, not before. The reviewer runs only in the cases listed in [when the reviewer runs](references/reviewer-use.md); read it the first time you consider calling the reviewer. How to start, wait on and pass methods to subagents is in [dispatch](references/dispatch.md); read it before the first dispatch.
%% codex
Every role is a subagent started with `spawn_agent`. Which model plays each role is resolved in [models](references/models.md). Read a reference when the step that names it comes up, not before. The reviewer runs only in the cases listed in [when the reviewer runs](references/reviewer-use.md); read it the first time you consider calling the reviewer. How to start, wait on and pass methods to subagents is in [dispatch](references/dispatch.md); read it before the first dispatch.
%% end

## Steps

%% cc
1. **Preflight.** Confirm this is a git repository (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`) and record the full baseline SHA. If it isn't, stop and say so. Resolve the models per [models](references/models.md).
%% codex
1. **Preflight.** Confirm this is a git repository (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`) and record the full baseline SHA, and that `spawn_agent` and `wait_agent` are available in this session (they need the `multi_agent` feature). If either check fails, stop and say so. Resolve the models per [models](references/models.md); without a config, stop and point to `native-routed-setup-{host}`.
%% end
2. **Scope.** In your first reply, tell the user in your own words the goal, the problem it solves and the smallest change you expect to meet it, then carry on without waiting; if the evidence rules out what the user asked for, show it and stop. Restate the scope and a **checkable done-condition** in two or three sentences, and write them to `~/.native-routed/notes/<topic>/scope.md`, outside the repo. Record the resolved models there, and copy `references/tests.md` (test discipline), `references/review.md` (review method) and `references/principles.md` (design principles) into the same directory with `cp`, without reading `tests.md` or `review.md`: workers and the reviewer follow the copies in the notes. Write `session.json` in the same directory as [dispatch](references/dispatch.md#session-record) describes, so `native-watch-{host}` can find this run. Ask the user only when the scope is too vague to write a done-condition. Start `scope.md` with one keyword line naming the features, samples, modules and repositories the task touches, so a later search by any of them finds this directory. Name in the done-condition the surface the user checks (a command or page in the main checkout), not an agent's report. Keep `progress.md` in the same directory as the resume point: intent, current step, what is verified, agents and worktrees still live, project gates still open, next steps, key files and gotchas; rewrite it at every unit boundary and keep it under 40 lines. Agents append to `log.md` beside it; `progress.md` is yours alone. When a brief defers a project's delivery gate (a review page, a sync, a publish step), list the gate in `progress.md` until it is met, and report it as not verified if the task ends first.
   At any later fork, first ask whether running something would answer it (behaviour, output, layout, timing, whether a check separates); if so, dispatch a worker to try it and let the result decide. Ask the user only for a product or preference call no experiment settles. Otherwise proceed on a sensible default, and say which one and what the user could tell you to do instead.
3. **Split into work units**, each with a goal, acceptance checks and a file scope.
   - Several similar units: take the first through brief, implementation, review and acceptance before dispatching the rest, so a flaw in the brief costs one agent, not all of them.
   - When the code or docs need to be understood first, or the web searched, dispatch the worker for read-only exploration or research, then split the implementation from its evidence. A pure research task is accepted on its deliverable.
   - One unit, or units touching the same files: one worker does them serially in the current working tree.
%% cc
   - Independent units with disjoint files: one worker per unit, each started with `isolation: "worktree"`. A separate worker merges the branches and re-runs the relevant tests; no conflicts is not the same as verified. Add every worktree to `session.json`.
%% codex
   - Independent units with disjoint files: one worker per unit, each in its own worktree and branch that this session creates with `git worktree add` before dispatch (see [dispatch](references/dispatch.md#worktrees)). A separate worker merges the branches and re-runs the relevant tests; no conflicts is not the same as verified. Add every worktree to `session.json`.
%% end
4. **Brief.** Before writing any brief, read [brief rules](references/brief.md): contents, baseline refresh, the decided design, the pre-flight, what the agent delivers (including the write scope and `wait-job.sh`), the stop-condition table, and taking over or resuming an agent. When the brief carries a decided design, also read the [design principles](references/principles.md) (the copy in the notes). A brief names the notes path and, for each repository the agent writes to, that worktree's absolute path. A subagent sees none of this conversation, so the brief must stand on its own.
%% codex
   Subagents are started with `fork_turns: "none"`. Every brief also says: don't start subagents of your own.
%% end
5. **Review.** After implementation, start a fresh reviewer subagent on the reviewer model with a read-only brief: follow the review method and design principles copies in the notes, from the baseline SHA to the candidate, with the confirmed requirement and the notes as the Spec source. Afterwards check `git status` in the reviewed tree shows no changes. Every other case that calls the reviewer is in [when the reviewer runs](references/reviewer-use.md). Author and reviewer are never the same agent. Before acting on a P0–P2 behaviour or Spec finding, rerun its command yourself: keep it only when the output shows the failure, otherwise move it to the user list with the output you got. Hand the kept P0–P2 findings to a **fresh** worker; the fix is done when the same commands print the required output. Extras added because they might help, and changes motivated by a hypothesis the evidence refuted, are reverted, not shipped. If the fix touched production code paths, have a fresh reviewer review the fix diff or say in the report that it was not re-reviewed. List P3 and Unproven findings for the user to decide; do not auto-fix them.
6. **Retry and repeated failure.** Retry a failed agent by cause: a smaller scope after a context or memory limit, the same brief after a dropped connection, a different model after a tool error, per [model rejected mid-run](references/models.md#model-rejected-mid-run). When the same failure happens a second time, or two retries didn't help, don't let the implementer retry in place. Write down the premise the failed attempts share, then convene the committee: start one read-only subagent per committee entry, all in parallel, each with the shared premise, the failure evidence, the notes path and the baseline SHA. Alternate two briefs down the list, the first member testing the premise directly and digging for the root cause and the second challenging the current approach; each returns a diagnosis and a plan. Compare them, pick or merge a plan, record it in the notes, then dispatch a fresh worker.
7. **Resume.** Subagents don't outlive this session. On every "continue" or after a restart, read `~/.native-routed/notes/<topic>/`, starting with `progress.md`, then check `git log` and `git status` in the main tree and every worktree in `session.json` before doing anything else. After a context compaction, read [compaction](references/compaction.md) first. The notes and git history are authoritative: don't redo verified work, but re-verify on the real artifact any inherited claim the next step relies on.

## Acceptance

Read [acceptance](references/acceptance.md) when the first agent returns and apply it on every return of an agent; it holds the checks, the cleanup of worktrees, the verdicts and the rule for counts. A review verdict covers one head SHA; a later commit needs a new verdict. If a fix touched production code paths, have a fresh reviewer review the fix diff or say in the report that it was not re-reviewed.

## Report

When wrapping up, read and follow [report](references/report.md). Also name every model fallback.
